# QA Configuration
#
# Copy this file to .opencode/qa.conf (never commit qa.conf itself).
#
# Grammar: Blank lines and lines starting with # are skipped. Every other line
# splits on its first = into key and value, with no whitespace trimming and no
# quote stripping. Unknown keys are ignored. The last occurrence of a key wins.
# A final line without a trailing newline is still read. A value carrying a
# carriage return or other control character (a file saved with CRLF line
# endings, for one) makes its entries malformed.
#
# Origins: Each entry is shaped scheme://host[:port]. Scheme is http or https,
# required. No path. Host is lower-cased before comparison and held to the same
# rules as a URL host. A leading *. is allowed and matches any subdomain at any
# depth (*.example.com matches a.example.com and a.b.example.com, but not
# example.com itself). A port :* matches any port; an entry without a port
# matches only the scheme's default port (80 for http, 443 for https).
#
# QA_ALLOWED_ORIGINS: space-separated list. If set, replaces the default
# allowlist (http://localhost:*, https://localhost:*, http://127.0.0.1:*,
# https://127.0.0.1:*, http://[::1]:*, https://[::1]:*). Set but empty allows
# only about:blank. Malformed entries deny every Ripley call until fixed.
#
# QA_BLOCKED_ORIGINS: space-separated list. A blocked match always wins, even
# if the origin is on the allowlist. Malformed entries deny every Ripley call
# until fixed. Production URLs belong here.
#
# Examples (commented out):
#QA_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:* http://127.0.0.1:* https://staging.example.com
#QA_BLOCKED_ORIGINS=https://example.com https://www.example.com
